  "before_interfaces": {
    "thm-orthogonal-transform-preserves-standard-normal-product-law": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z\\sim N_n(0,I_n)$ have independent standard-normal coordinates, and let $U\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ satisfy $U^TU=I_n$. Then $UZ$ also has independent $N(0,1)$ coordinates. Here $I_n$ is the identity matrix and $U$ is orthogonal."
    ],
    "def-chi-square-law": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. For an integer $k\\ge1$, let $G=(G_1,\\ldots,G_k)$ have law $N_k(0,I_k)$, so its coordinates are independent $N(0,1)$ random variables. The **chi-square law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi_k^2$, is the law of\n$$\nQ_k=\\sum_{j=1}^k G_j^2=\\|G\\|^2.\n$$\nFor $k=0$, define $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$, the point mass at zero. This zero-degree law records a rank-zero projection only; it is not used as a denominator in a Student or Fisher ratio. The multivariate-normal characteristic-function theorem makes the definition independent of the chosen realization of $G$."
    ],
    "lem-positive-df-chi-square-has-density-and-regular-cdf": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. For every integer $k\\ge1$, the law $\\chi_k^2$ has density\n$$\nf_k(x)=c_k x^{k/2-1}e^{-x/2}\\mathbf 1_{\\{x>0\\}},\\qquad c_k=\\frac{\\sigma_{k-1}(S^{k-1})}{2(2\\pi)^{k/2}}>0,\n$$\nwhere $\\sigma_{k-1}$ is the polar surface measure on the unit sphere in $\\mathbb R^k$. Consequently, $Q_k>0$ almost surely, and its cumulative distribution function is continuous on $\\mathbb R$ and strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$."
    ],
    "thm-orthogonal-projection-has-independent-chi-square-sums": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $n\\ge1$, let $Z\\sim N_n(0,I_n)$, and let $P\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times n}$ be an orthogonal projection of rank $r$, where $0\\le r\\le n$. Then the random vectors $PZ$ and $(I_n-P)Z$ are independent, and\n$$\n\\|PZ\\|^2\\sim\\chi_r^2,\\qquad \\|(I_n-P)Z\\|^2\\sim\\chi_{n-r}^2.\n$$\nThe two squared norms are independent. The cases $r=0$ and $r=n$ are included, with $\\chi_0^2=\\delta_0$."
    ],
    "def-student-t-and-f-laws": [
      "## Definition\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. For a positive integer $k$, take the product probability law of an independent standard normal $G$ and an independent $Q_k\\sim\\chi_k^2$. The **Student t law with $k$ degrees of freedom**, denoted $t_k$, is the law of\n$$\nT_k=\\frac{G}{\\sqrt{Q_k/k}}.\n$$\nFor positive integers $r,s$, take independent $Q_r\\sim\\chi_r^2$ and $Q_s\\sim\\chi_s^2$. The **Fisher F law with degrees $(r,s)$**, denoted $F_{r,s}$, is the law of\n$$\nF_{r,s}=\\frac{Q_r/r}{Q_s/s}.\n$$\nThe positive-degree chi-square lemma proves that the denominators are positive almost surely, so these are real-valued random variables with well-defined laws. Zero degrees are excluded from both definitions."
    ],
    "lem-student-t-and-f-cdfs-are-continuous-and-strictly-increasing": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. For every $k\\ge1$, the CDF of $t_k$ is continuous and strictly increasing on $\\mathbb R$, is symmetric about zero, and has limits $0$ and $1$ at $-\\infty$ and $+\\infty$. For every $r,s\\ge1$, the CDF of $F_{r,s}$ is continuous on $\\mathbb R$, is zero on $(-\\infty,0]$, and is strictly increasing on $(0,\\infty)$ with limit $1$ at $+\\infty$. Consequently, for each $u\\in(0,1)$ each law has exactly one $u$-quantile on its support."
    ],
    "thm-normal-mean-residual-independence-and-exact-t-pivot": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Use the experiment with data space $(\\mathbb R^n,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n))$, parameter set $\\mathbb R\\times(0,\\infty)$, and sampling law $P_{\\mu,\\sigma^2}=N(\\mu,\\sigma^2)^{\\otimes n}$. Let $n\\ge2$, $\\sigma>0$, and let $X_1,\\ldots,X_n$ be the independent coordinates of the observation. Define\n$$\n\\bar X=\\frac1n\\sum_{i=1}^nX_i,\\qquad S^2=\\frac1{n-1}\\sum_{i=1}^n(X_i-\\bar X)^2,\\qquad S=\\sqrt{S^2}.\n$$\nThe map $x\\mapsto\\bar x$ is a statistic. The sample mean $\\bar X$ and residual sum $S^2$ are independent,\n$$\n\\frac{(n-1)S^2}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi_{n-1}^2,\\qquad S>0\\ \\text{almost surely},\\qquad \\frac{\\bar X-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt n}\\sim t_{n-1}.\n$$\nFor every $0<\\alpha<1$, the interval\n$$\n\\left[\\bar X-q_{n-1}(1-\\alpha/2)\\frac{S}{\\sqrt n},\\ \\bar X+q_{n-1}(1-\\alpha/2)\\frac{S}{\\sqrt n}\\right]\n$$\nhas exact coverage $1-\\alpha$, where $q_{n-1}(u)$ is the unique $u$-quantile of $t_{n-1}$ established in [[lem-student-t-and-f-cdfs-are-continuous-and-strictly-increasing]]."
    ],
    "thm-gaussian-linear-model-qr-and-exact-t-f-pivots": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Fix a deterministic matrix $X\\in\\mathbb R^{n\\times p}$ with $n>p\\ge1$ whose $p$ columns are linearly independent. Use the experiment on $(\\mathbb R^n,\\mathcal B(\\mathbb R^n))$ indexed by $(\\beta,\\sigma^2)\\in\\mathbb R^p\\times(0,\\infty)$, with observation $Y=X\\beta+\\varepsilon$ and independent errors $\\varepsilon_i\\sim N(0,\\sigma^2)$. Define the least-squares statistic and residual sum of squares by\n$$\n\\widehat\\beta=(X^TX)^{-1}X^TY,\\qquad P_X=X(X^TX)^{-1}X^T,\\qquad RSS_X=\\|(I_n-P_X)Y\\|^2,\\qquad S^2=\\frac{RSS_X}{n-p}.\n$$\nFor every nonzero $c\\in\\mathbb R^p$,\n$$\n\\frac{c^T\\widehat\\beta-c^T\\beta}{S\\sqrt{c^T(X^TX)^{-1}c}}\\sim t_{n-p},\\qquad \\frac{RSS_X}{\\sigma^2}\\sim\\chi_{n-p}^2,\n$$\nand $\\widehat\\beta$ is independent of $RSS_X$, with $S>0$ almost surely.\n\nFor nested deterministic full and reduced design spaces $W_R\\subset W_U\\subseteq\\mathbb R^n$ of dimensions $p-q$ and $p$, where $q$ is an integer and $1\\le q\\le p<n$, let $P_R,P_U$ be the orthogonal projections onto these spaces and define $RSS_R=\\|(I-P_R)Y\\|^2$ and $RSS_U=\\|(I-P_U)Y\\|^2$. Under the null hypothesis that the mean vector belongs to $W_R$,\n$$\n\\frac{(RSS_R-RSS_U)/q}{RSS_U/(n-p)}\\sim F_{q,n-p}.\n$$\nThe numerator and denominator chi-square contributions are independent. All t and F denominator degrees of freedom in the statement are positive."
    ],
    "ex-one-sample-unknown-variance-t-interval": [],
